{
  "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
  "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nJEFFREY L. FISHER, ESQ., appointed by this Court,\nStanford, California; on behalf of Petitioner.\nGARY K. KING, ESQ., Attorney General, Santa Fe, New\nMexico; on behalf of Respondent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Jeffrey L. Fisher)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The text, purpose, and history of the\nConfrontation Clause make it clear that the prosecution\ncannot introduce one person's testimonial statements\nthrough the in-court testimony of someone else. Thus,\nhaving held in Melendez-Diaz that a lab analyst's\nstatements in a forensic lab report are testimonial,\nthis is an easy case.\nThe State violated the Confrontation Clause\nby introducing lab analyst Curtis Caylor's statements in\na forensic lab report without putting him on the stand."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: We will hear\nargument first this morning in Case 09-10876,\nBullcoming v. New Mexico.\nMr. Fisher."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Fisher, when you say\n\"in the presence,\" do you -- do you mean it necessarily\nmust be in the courtroom, or would a video-conferencing\nset-up be permissible so that the technician or the\nanalyst could testify from the lab, rather -- but it\nwould be screened in -- in the courthouse?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Jeffrey L. Fisher)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0000-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But a surrogate witness procedure violates\nall four components of the right to confrontation. It\nquite obviously violates the defendant's right to have\nthe witness testify in his presence, in the presence of\nthe jury so the jury can observe it, and under oath, as\nhappened in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Jeffrey L. Fisher)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now -- now, there is an amicus brief in the\ncase, I believe, that suggests some flexibility that\ntrial judges might employ in -- in accommodating lab\nanalysts' schedules."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The State could make that choice, but it would be a considerably weaker case, Justice Alito, not just because they wouldn't have anyone saying that the procedures were actually followed in this case, but also in this case it would be a far weaker case because they'd have somebody on the stand who would now be subject to cross-examination as to why he had been recently put on unpaid leave."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "How do you answer the practical situation that Caylor, who did this particular run, does some dozens day in and day out, and he will have no memory, in fact, of this particular test. So having him there -- he knows how the process operates; he doesn't remember this particular one -- how does having him there -- what could be elicited on cross-examination of him that couldn't be from his supervisor?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As the Court said in Michigan v. Bryant, a police interrogation resembles an ex parte examination when the primary purpose of the examination is to create, quote, an out-of-court substitute for trial testimony, end quote. A public record not prepared by a police officer is not the product of structured interrogation. It is neither ex parte nor is it an examination."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This analyst was fired, as I recall. Was he fired, placed on administrative leave or something?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. King",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KING: Your Honor, I don't believe that that is the only purpose that this form 705 could be used for. It is the common purpose for this form, but it is a form that the lab uses in every circumstance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why is a key question whether this was an interrogation? Does the Confrontation Clause apply only to confrontations? To interrogations?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. King",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KING: Procedurally, in this case, Your Honor, this -- the trial at the District Court level occurred before the Court's opinion in Melendez-Diaz."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. King",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KING: I think that you have to do the analysis in each kind of statement that you're looking at, Your Honor, to determine whether or not the statement that's -- that's being made and that's being proposed for trial is a substitute for live in-court testimony."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. King",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0003-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the gas chromatograph gave us a printout that said that the -- that the level of alcohol in the blood is .21 grams per 100 milliliters, Mr. Caylor transferred that to a form, and that's what we are putting in."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0003-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: But you -- you -- you think the result would be a -- the same if we could develop a machine that you put the bullet in and -- and -- and -\u00ad that's been fired from this gun, and the murder bullet, and the machine goes, blah, blah, blah, and it spits out, you know, 99 percent, 99.9 percent match, that would be okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e74f1b44bab33e1cfdcb32d78a63fd083cd4ab9795662610e5d05e2bb2ee910a",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e74f1b44bab3-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Do the rules of criminal -- do the rules of criminal procedure in New Mexico say it should be prima facie evidence?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2010-09-10876",
      "confidence": 0.0,
